cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Autopoietic Code Rewriting inside Isolated Execution Environments"
message: "If you use this research or implementation, please cite as below."
type: software
version: "0.1.0-research-draft"
date-released: "2026-10-31"
license: MIT
abstract: |
  This research investigates the conditions under which a software system
  can modify its own source-code representation during runtime while
  preserving structural integrity, execution continuity, and behavioral
  consistency. The initial scope is restricted to individual top-level
  Python functions using LibCST and multiprocessing-based logical isolation.
  Four controlled transformation classes are considered: constant folding,
  algebraic simplification, dead branch elimination, and local variable
  renaming. The study separates four operational properties: execution
  isolation, transformation validity, deployment integrity, and
  recoverability. State Restoration Fidelity is introduced as a separate
  evaluation dimension from rollback success.
keywords:
  - autonomous software modification
  - runtime code rewriting
  - abstract syntax trees
  - program transformation
  - structural invariants
  - differential testing
  - runtime verification
  - dynamic software updating
  - recoverability
